---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { catalogCategories } from '../../lib/catalog';
---
<BaseLayout title="Oopbuy Spreadsheet Categories: Find Products Faster (2026) | OopbuyTop" description="Browse Oopbuy spreadsheet categories for shoes, clothing, bags, accessories and more. Use category pages to compare current finds and QC availability.">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Oopbuy spreadsheet categories</p>
    <h1>Oopbuy Spreadsheet Categories: Find Products Faster</h1>
    <p>A category-first spreadsheet is easier to use than a long, unfiltered list. Instead of opening unrelated links one by one, begin with the item type you actually want to research. That puts comparable titles, images, price context and QC-photo indicators in one place.</p>
    <p>Category pages are discovery tools, not product approval pages. Use them to form a short research list, then open the current listing to verify stock, selected option, measurements, seller details and final pricing.</p>

    <h2>Why start with a category?</h2>
    <p>A single product name can cover many versions, sellers and quality levels. When you review similar items side by side, it is easier to notice differences in titles, visible construction, image coverage and price ranges. This is more useful than treating the first search result as the answer.</p>
    <p>Category browsing also gives your research a clear stopping point. Pick one category, compare a small number of candidates, save the best few and then move to live verification. It is a calmer process than accumulating dozens of open tabs.</p>

    <h2>How to use an Oopbuy spreadsheet category page</h2>
    <h3>1. Choose the most specific useful category</h3>
    <p>Start with the product family: sneakers, outerwear, bags, accessories, audio or another visible category. If the category is broad, use the titles and brand labels on the cards to narrow your short list. Do not force a product into a category just because the name is familiar; confirm it on the live page.</p>
    <h3>2. Compare three to five cards, not fifty</h3>
    <p>Look at the title, displayed price context, product image and QC-photo marker across a small group. A QC marker means that warehouse reference images are indexed for that card; it is not a score and does not certify the product. Select the few listings worth opening rather than trying to assess everything in the grid.</p>
    <h3>3. Open the current listing before acting</h3>
    <p>Listings may change after the spreadsheet data was captured. Check the live title, price, available sizes or variants, seller information and all relevant photos. If the listing no longer matches the card, treat the live listing as current and continue researching rather than relying on an old snapshot.</p>
    <h3>4. Keep notes that answer a decision</h3>
    <p>For each candidate, note the exact variant, visible measurements or material claims, what you still need to check and the date you looked at it. These notes are more useful than saving only a link, especially when several similar listings exist.</p>

    <h2>Browse the current categories</h2>
    <p>These category pages group the currently indexed finds. Counts and products can change as the catalogue updates; use them as a route into comparison, then verify the live listing.</p>
    <div class="category-grid">{catalogCategories.slice(0, 24).map((category) => <a class="category-card" href={`/category/${category.slug}/`}><strong>{category.name}</strong><span>{category.productCount ? `${category.productCount.toLocaleString()} current finds` : 'Browse finds'} →</span></a>)}</div>

    <h2>What to compare inside a category</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Title and variant:</strong> check that the wording describes the specific option you want.</li>
      <li><strong>Images:</strong> look for angles that show the product details you care about, not only a polished cover image.</li>
      <li><strong>Price context:</strong> use the range as a prompt to compare listings, not as a final quote.</li>
      <li><strong>QC availability:</strong> use available reference photos as another research input, then examine the images themselves.</li>
      <li><strong>Live details:</strong> confirm stock, sizes, terms and seller claims on the listing before placing an order.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Category research FAQ</h2>
    <h3>Does a category page show every available product?</h3>
    <p>No. It shows the finds currently indexed by this resource. The source catalogue and live listings can change, so it should not be treated as a complete market search.</p>
    <h3>Why is a product card different from the live listing?</h3>
    <p>Price, stock, images and listing text can change. The card is a starting point for discovery; the live listing is where you confirm the current details.</p>
    <h3>Is a QC-photo count a quality rating?</h3>
    <p>No. It only indicates how many warehouse reference images are associated with the indexed card. Review the actual images and make your own decision.</p>
    <p><a class="text-link" href="/categories/">Open all spreadsheet categories →</a></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
